  "preamble": "The shift from the voiceless alveolar stop [t] to its voiced counterpart [d] is a primary form of lenition. It typically occurs in intervocalic positions where the vocal folds continue to vibrate through the consonant, assimilating the stop to the surrounding vowels. Western Armenian voiced the Classical Armenian plain voiceless stops, so տ /t/ became [d] (տուն 'house' [dun]), while Eastern Armenian keeps [t].",
  "phoneticEffects": "Voicing, Lenition",
